db/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192712 #edb/ref 1039 "Approximating Volumes of Revolution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192712 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192712 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ea7448d8-a235-52f4-a475-006943f8a142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1045 "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192870 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "15829972-de8f-5398-aedc-ee127d53c955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193027 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [1,4],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (1,4).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)=x\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4]$ using a left Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbf952c0-e985-5c40-a219-a70a8e942eb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193027 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,4),$ we have \n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193530 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193530 #edb/ref 1061 39566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1997ebaf-5bad-5fd7-875b-27650eb3f5b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193531 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193531 #edb/ref 1061 39567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193531 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99cb3798-47dd-5790-b2f7-fdfbcde6185f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193531 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193532 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193532 #edb/ref 1061 39568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193532 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7001e0a2-bc0e-5f22-adfb-6688e2d5c0c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193532 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193533 #edb/ref 1061 39569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193533 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d04db767-8034-5d9f-8871-0fb87a4da642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193533 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193546 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193546 #edb/ref 1061 39706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193546 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9769694-1018-5682-8dd1-facf2627a6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193546 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193547 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193547 #edb/ref 1061 39707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cc57411-9166-578d-ad5d-18fd5906fee2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193694 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193694 #edb/ref 1061 44209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193694 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ed00cb2-4d94-5a81-8e1c-488592b9d60c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193694 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193695 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193695 #edb/ref 1061 44210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4da87fab-f669-5510-93d3-55fea59cbf65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193695 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193827 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to use Riemann sums to approximate definite integrals of *composite* functions. As we'll see, this has some interesting applications.\n\nFor example, suppose $f(x)$ is a positive, continuous function. Some values for $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [1,5]$ are given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nLet's use this data to approximate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo approximate this definite integral, we first define a new function $g(x)$ as\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2\\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nNow, our task is to approximate\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals.\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5-1}{4} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx (g(1) + g(2) + g(3) + g(4)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nLet's add a row to our table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $g(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $9$ | $16$ | $50$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{1}^{5}g(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx (3 + 8 + 9 + 16) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a right Riemann sum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193827 #edb/ref 1065 6553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193827 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2502b79-d3d8-53e4-be62-b01752a16be9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193827 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193831 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to use Riemann sums to approximate areas under curves of composite functions. Our final task is determining whether these approximations overestimate or underestimate the true areas.\n\nWe start by considering *right* Riemann sums. Recall the following facts:\n\n- If a function is strictly *increasing*, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If a function is strictly *decreasing*, the right Riemann sum *underestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x \\in [1,2]$, and differentiable for $x \\in (1,2)$. Now, consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nSuppose we use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve $y = g(x)$ over $x\\in [1,2]$. Does the right Riemann sum overestimate or underestimate the true area?\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Differentiating our expression for $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n$g^{′}(x)|= (x^{2})^{′} \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x); = 2x \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x)$.\nWe now examine the sign of each factor in this expression to determine the sign of $g'(x)$.Since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,2)$, we have\n$2x \\gt 0, \\qquad f(x) \\gt 0, \\qquad x^2 \\gt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$g'(x) = \\underbrace{2x}_{\\gt 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2}_{> 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{2x \\cdot f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2 \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} \\gt 0$.\n\nTherefore, since $g'(x) \\gt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a left Riemann sum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193831 #edb/ref 1065 6582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193831 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4017a527-6912-5d2d-a92d-3424c1399d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193831 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining Overestimates and Underestimates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193904 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/59/59f9a4f76889bf4323c7bcf7dad2dd5269698d94f294458eafdd4c5da0fa0580.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis (called the **axis of revolution**), we get a **solid of revolution,** as shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc2858aac81addf1d5c19cd3836361d42de4ce844117ab4d42960e45a274026d.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the volume $V$ of this solid of revolution is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193904 #edb/ref 1065 7613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193904 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "169b0392-4eed-5202-921f-c379f4264c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193904 #edb/ref 1067 "Volumes of Revolution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193905 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$.\n\n![](images/e7/e7fb3f6e11e0e2b51fc4ecae9827a4e378a7981450775f2b9330057c319688bf.png)\n\nThe length of the curve between these two points is known as the **arc length** of the curve.\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length is given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can use Riemann sums to approximate arc lengths. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193905 #edb/ref 1065 7614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193905 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c6c4aa50-1ce8-52f8-aa97-b2bce6c85623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193905 #edb/ref 1067 "Arc Length" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186510490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186510511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186510514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186510539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186510553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1045 "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186510514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509689 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b6c8f1ee-bd3b-5b8f-a3ec-29aa9a0902db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509718 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509718 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509718 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f63c264-1a0f-552a-b8ad-9620bc2188c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509722 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13fe11c3-e0ca-520e-8103-0b01e9b72896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509725 #edb/ref 1080 "48\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509725 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509725 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14818b9f-c464-5b94-bee6-e801c16f016a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509737 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509737 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509737 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "190b0318-472c-530a-9953-285c1d80e520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509767 #edb/ref 1080 "11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509767 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509767 #edb/ref 1083 #uu